Week 3 Review — Production RAG (Q&A Style)

A compiled revision document from the Week 3 Q&A session on production RAG: ingestion, chunking, retrieval, vector databases, and the project.
Topic 1: Document Ingestion
The pipeline: Documents → Parsing → Cleaning → Metadata → Chunking → Embedding → Vector DB.
Parsing comes before cleaning. A file is a format, not just text. Parsing understands the container (PDF, HTML, Markdown) and extracts the content. Only then can cleaning remove the noise left in the extracted text.
Cleaning. Removes repeated headers and footers, page numbers, broken mid-sentence line breaks, and leftover artifacts like bullet symbols or table borders. Skip it and that junk gets embedded too, which dilutes each chunk's meaning and hurts retrieval.
Metadata. Source, page number, author, dates, document ID and version. It powers citations and filtering later, for example "only this document" or "only the latest version".
PDFs are the hardest. A PDF stores characters at coordinates on a page, not paragraphs, so the parser has to guess reading order.
• Two-column layouts read straight across produce sentences stitched from unrelated paragraphs. Fix: a layout-aware parser that reads each column top to bottom.
• Scanned PDFs are images, so they need OCR to get any text.
• Tables flattened into plain text lose the link between a value and its row and column headers ("Q1 450 Q2 520 North South" is ambiguous). Fix: extract tables separately and convert them to Markdown or to sentences like "North region, Q1 sales: 450".
HTML and Markdown already mark headings, lists and tables explicitly, so structure is read directly instead of guessed. It also pays off in chunking, since you can split at heading boundaries and keep the section title as metadata. For HTML, cleaning means stripping navigation menus, ads, scripts and footers.
Document versioning. If a policy changes and old chunks stay in the database, the system confidently answers from the old version, or mixes old and new into one contradictory answer. Each chunk carries a document ID, version, and last-updated date. On change, either delete the old chunks and insert new ones, or mark old ones inactive and filter them out at query time.
Detecting changes cheaply. A periodic job computes a content hash (a fingerprint) per document and compares it with the hash stored in metadata. Same hash: skip. Different hash: re-parse, re-chunk, re-embed, replace. Only changed documents get reprocessed, which saves embedding cost.
import hashlib

def content_hash(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()

def needs_reingest(doc_id: str, new_text: str, stored_hashes: dict) -> bool:
    return stored_hashes.get(doc_id) != content_hash(new_text)

Topic 2: Chunking
Why chunk at all. One vector can't capture the meaning of a 200-page document, and the whole thing wouldn't fit in the LLM's context anyway. So documents are split into pieces that are embedded and retrieved individually.
Strategy
How it splits
Use when
Cost or catch
Fixed-size
Every N tokens (e.g. 500) with overlap (e.g. 50)
Baseline, prototypes, uniform or messy text
Cuts blindly, can slice an idea in half
Recursive
