Fixed-size and overlap. If a cut lands mid-sentence, chunk one ends with half a thought and chunk two starts with the other half, so neither carries the full meaning. Overlap repeats the last 50 tokens of chunk one at the start of chunk two, so a sentence near the boundary appears whole in at least one chunk. The cost is some duplicated tokens.
Recursive. A paragraph usually holds one complete idea, so keep paragraphs intact. Only if a paragraph exceeds the size limit does it drop to sentences, and only if a sentence is too big does it split on words. It cuts hard only as a last resort.
Semantic. Embed each sentence, compare neighbours with cosine similarity, and cut where similarity drops sharply. Keywords are a weaker signal, since two sentences can mean the same thing with different words. It is worth the cost only when topics shift often and structure is missing. (Not to be confused with document-aware chunking, which splits using a document's own headings.)
Parent-child. Small chunks give sharp embeddings that match questions accurately. Big chunks give the LLM enough context to answer fully. Parent-child gets both: search small, return big. Note it doesn't make search cheaper, since there are more chunks overall. The win is precision.
Rule of thumb. Start with recursive, measure retrieval quality, and move to something fancier only when you can show it helps.
from langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter

splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(
    chunk_size=500,
    chunk_overlap=50,
    separators=["\n\n", "\n", ". ", " ", ""],  # paragraph, line, sentence, word, char
)
chunks = splitter.split_text(cleaned_text)
The sizes above are in characters by default. To count tokens instead, use the splitter's tiktoken-based constructor. Verify the import path against the current LangChain docs before running.

Topic 3: Retrieval
The full architecture: Query → Query transformation → Hybrid retrieval (BM25 + vector search, with a metadata pre-filter) → Merge (RRF) → Reranker → Top K → LLM.
Dense vs. sparse. Dense retrieval embeds the query and chunks and finds the closest in meaning. Sparse retrieval (BM25) matches the actual words, scoring by how often and how rarely the query's terms appear.
• "ERR-4021" has no semantic meaning for an embedding model, so dense may return generic error chunks. BM25 matches the exact token. Sparse wins.
• "How do I get my money back?" vs. a document saying "refund policy" shares no words, but the embeddings land close. Dense wins.
• Each method covers the other's blind spot, which is the whole case for hybrid.
Merging with Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). You can't add raw scores: BM25 is unbounded (12, 30) while cosine similarity sits around 0.3 to 0.9, so BM25 would swamp the sum. RRF ignores raw scores and uses only rank positions. For each list a chunk appears in, it earns 1 / (60 + rank). Add those up across lists. The 60 is a smoothing constant.
Example: chunk A is rank 1 in BM25 and rank 3 in vector search, so 1/61 + 1/63 ≈ 0.0323. Chunk B is rank 2 in BM25 and missing from vector, so 1/62 ≈ 0.0161. A wins because it ranks well in both lists.
def rrf(ranked_lists: list[list[str]], k: int = 60) -> list[str]:
    scores: dict[str, float] = {}
    for ranking in ranked_lists:
        for rank, doc_id in enumerate(ranking, start=1):
            scores[doc_id] = scores.get(doc_id, 0.0) + 1.0 / (k + rank)
    return sorted(scores, key=scores.get, reverse=True)

merged = rrf([bm25_ids, vector_ids])
Metadata filtering: before, not after. Pre-filter so you search a smaller pool and rank within it. Post-filtering can leave you with zero results, because the top 5 may all be from old versions while good current chunks sat further down. Typical filters: document version, source, department, date range, and user permissions (never show one customer's documents to another).
Query rewriting. A vague query like "it's not working, what do I do?" gives a vague embedding and gives BM25 almost nothing to match. An LLM rewrites it using chat context, expands abbreviations, fixes typos, and adds likely keywords. "What about the premium plan?" becomes "What is the refund policy for the premium plan?".
Multi-query retrieval. The LLM generates several phrasings (e.g. "cancel subscription", "terminate account", "stop billing") and you retrieve for each, then merge with RRF. This improves coverage, since the documents may phrase the answer in a way one rewrite never guessed. Cost: an extra LLM call and several retrievals.
Reranking. First-stage retrieval embeds query and chunk separately, so they never see each other. A cross-encoder reads the query and chunk together and scores relevance more accurately. It runs once per candidate, so it is too slow for millions of chunks. Hence two stages: cheap retrieval narrows to about 20, the reranker picks the best 5.
from sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder

reranker = CrossEncoder("cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2")
pairs = [(query, chunk.text) for chunk in candidates]
scores = reranker.predict(pairs)
top_k = [c for _, c in sorted(zip(scores, candidates), key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)][:5]
Contextual compression. Even the best chunks hold filler. Compression trims each chunk to the relevant parts, via an LLM extracting key sentences or by filtering low-scoring sentences. It saves tokens, leaves room for more chunks, and reduces noise. Trade-off: possibly an extra LLM call, and a risk of cutting something important. Add it when context is genuinely bloated.
Cutting latency. Drop the extra LLM calls first: keep a single query rewrite instead of multi-query, and skip compression. Keep the reranker (biggest quality gain per millisecond) and the hybrid retrieval itself.
Topic 4: Vector Databases
The plan: stay lean. You know FAISS and Chroma. Add PostgreSQL + pgvector first, then learn one production vector DB later.
What FAISS and Chroma lack in production. FAISS is a library and a bare index, not a database. It can save an index to disk, but there is no easy update or delete handling, no metadata filtering, no access control, no backups. Chroma has storage and filtering but is built for simplicity and small to mid-sized setups, not the scale, reliability and operational tooling production needs.
Why pgvector. An extension that adds a vector column type and similarity search to PostgreSQL. Vectors live beside your users, documents and permissions tables, so you get:
• One database to run, back up and secure.
• Metadata filtering in plain SQL, in the same query as the vector search.
• Permission checks via joins against your users table.
• Transactions: deleting old chunks and inserting new ones is atomic.
Note: pgvector is dense only. For BM25-style keyword search in Postgres, use its built-in full-text search and merge the two result lists yourself (RRF).
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;

CREATE TABLE chunks (
    id           BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    doc_id       TEXT NOT NULL,
    version      INT NOT NULL,
    is_active    BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE,
    page         INT,
    content      TEXT NOT NULL,
    embedding    vector(1536)   -- dimension must match your embedding model
);

CREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);

-- Pre-filtered similarity search (<=> is cosine distance)
SELECT id, content
FROM chunks
WHERE is_active = TRUE AND doc_id = $1
ORDER BY embedding <=> $2
LIMIT 20;
Approximate nearest neighbour (ANN). Brute force compares the query to every vector: exact but slow at millions of vectors. ANN builds a structure in advance so a query checks only a promising region. Trade-off: a small loss of accuracy for a big gain in speed. For RAG that is fine, since you retrieve about 20 candidates and rerank anyway.
IVFFlat: sorting into piles. At build time, vectors are clustered into groups, each with a centre. At query time, compare the query to the centres, pick the nearest few clusters, and search only inside them. Lighter and faster to build, but recall is usually lower, because the true nearest vector may sit in a cluster you skipped (the boundary problem). It needs data in the table before you build the index, since it must see the vectors to form the clusters.
HNSW: a layered road map. A graph where each vector links to its nearest neighbours, with sparse upper layers holding long-distance links. A query starts at the top, hops toward the right area, drops a layer, refines, and finishes by walking between close neighbours on the bottom layer. Best speed and recall, but more memory and slower to build. Usual default.
Both are approximate. Only brute force is exact.
Beyond pgvector. Teams outgrow it on scale and operations, not because Postgres lacks cloud hosting (RDS, Aurora, Cloud SQL, Supabase and Neon mostly support pgvector). At hundreds of millions of vectors, HNSW indexes want RAM and builds get slow, and heavy vector traffic can compete with normal application queries.
